(Ord. of 3-1-2004(2), § 5-100.1)

676:
(a) Whenever the fire official shall find in any building or upon any premises dangerous or hazardous conditions or materials, as follows, there shall be issued such notice or orders to remove or remedy the conditions as may be necessary for the protection of life and property from fire and smoke:
(1) Dangerous or unlawful amounts of combustible or explosive or otherwise hazardous materials;
(2) Hazardous conditions arising from defective or improperly installed equipment for handling or using combustible or explosive or otherwise hazardous materials;
(3) Dangerous accumulations of rubbish, waste paper, boxes, shavings or other highly combustible materials;
(4) Accumulations of dust or waste material in air conditioning or ventilating systems or of grease in kitchen or other exhaust ducts or inadequate clearances to unprotected combustible material from hoods, grease extractors and ducts;
(5) Obstructions to or on fire escapes, designated access openings in exterior walls for fire department use, stairs, passageways, doors or windows, that could interfere with the operations of the fire department having jurisdiction or egress of occupants in case of fire;
(6) Any building or other structure which contains defects therein which increase the hazards of fire, accidents or other calamities, lacks adequate ventilation, light or sanitary facilities, or has conditions of dilapidation, disrepairs, structural defects or uncleanliness, lacks adequate exit facilities, automatic or other fire alarm apparatus or fire extinguishing equipment creates a hazardous condition.
(b) Standards and procedures.
(1) This article and the provisions of the standards codes adopted by the city in section 8-2 shall govern the standards and procedures for declaring and abating nuisances caused by the unsafe conditions as set forth in this division.
(2) The city official shall conduct a preliminary investigation of a dwelling, building or structure upon his own motion or by receipt of a request by a city department or five residents of the city, the requests charging that a dwelling, building or structure is unfit for human habitation or for commercial, industrial or business use. The building official shall make a preliminary investigation, and if a basis for such charges is found, the building official shall issue and cause to be served upon the owner and city council a notice as provided in the adopted standards codes.
(3) If the council determines at the public hearing per the procedure provided in the adopted standards codes that the dwelling, building or structure is unfit for human habitation or is unfit for its current commercial, industrial or business use it shall issue an order as provided in the adopted standards codes. In addition, the council shall state whether the repair, alteration or improvement of the dwelling, building or structure can be made at a reasonable cost in relation to the value of the dwelling, building or structure. If so, such repairs, alterations or improvements shall be made as ordered by the council. If the repair, alteration or improvement of the dwelling, building or structure cannot be made at a reasonable cost in relation to the value of the dwelling, building or structure, the council shall order the owner or parties in interest to remove or demolish such dwelling, building or structure. The city council shall not require removal or demolition of any dwelling, building or structure except upon a finding that the cost of repair, alteration or improvement thereof exceeds one-half the value such dwelling, building or structure will have when repaired so as to comply with the minimum requirements of this Code.
(c) When the building official is notified by the fire official that the corrections needed within a building are subject to the requirements of the building code, the building official shall notify the fire official when the corrections have been completed.
(Code 1985, § 12-3; Ord. of 3-4-1985; Ord. of 8-3-1992, §§ 1, 2)

